  "reasoning": "NO RED PHASE, same reasoning as T15: the observable result (Err + earlier write survives + later manifest unchanged) is identical pre- and post-refactor, since the same atomic_write failure point at tempfile::NamedTempFile::new_in fires whether triggered by write_version's old internal persist call or T03's new explicit one. Extended to a TWO-target plan (crate-a in the writable root, crate-b in a chmod-0o555 subdirectory) so that AC-011's 'entries earlier in the loop whose mutate-then-persist already succeeded remain persisted' clause is actually exercised, not merely asserted about a single-entry plan where there is no 'earlier entry' to check. Only crate-b's own subdirectory is chmod'd, not the workspace root, so crate-a's write can genuinely succeed. Round-3 fix (6), blocking-adjacent: the test process may run as uid 0 (root) in CI containers, where mode 0o555 does not block file creation at all -- the chmod would be a silent no-op, apply_version_plan would then succeed for both bumps, and this test's own failure-expecting assertions would themselves fail, breaking CI for a reason unrelated to this spec. A write-probe-and-early-return guard is added immediately after the chmod, before invoking apply_version_plan, to detect and skip this case rather than assume the chmod took effect. See `red_phase_waived_tasks`.",
