    "crates/callisto-graph/tests/apply_persist_open_count_test.rs"
  ],
  "reasoning": "NO RED PHASE (round-3 fix 1, blocking -- corrected from the prior revision's incorrect claim of genuine red). Placed in its own new integration-test binary, NOT inside apply.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module, because that module already contains other tests that call apply_version_plan/open() in the same test-binary process; open_call_count is a process-global counter, so those tests would pollute it even under #[serial] (which only mutexes against other #[serial] tests in the SAME binary, not against non-serial tests). Mirrors the exact precedent in crates/callisto-graph/tests/manifest_cache_test.rs:71-85 (dedicated file, #[serial], sole caller of open_call_count/reset_open_call_count in that binary). open_call_count() == 2 is an invariant of this WHOLE spec, not new behavior it introduces: apply.rs has exactly two `open(&decl, &ctx)` call sites (one in the bumps loop at ~apply.rs:82, one in the rewrites loop at ~apply.rs:113), confirmed by direct inspection of the live file, both present before AND after every task in this plan -- non_goals item 1 states explicitly that open/mutate/persist counts do not change. The pre-file-exists `error: no test target named ...` cargo error is a target-resolution artifact of the file not existing yet, not evidence the assertion itself can fail for the right reason -- once the file exists, the assertion passes on its very first execution, because the invariant it checks was never at risk from this spec's changes. Red-phase gate is explicitly waived for this task; see `red_phase_waived_tasks`.",
