{
  "id": "SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002",
  "purpose": "Change apply_version_plan (crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs) so that when multiple plan.bumps'/plan.rewrites' Manifest-trait-routed writes (VersionWriteTarget::Manifest / DepWriteTarget::Manifest) target the same physical manifest path, that path's manifest handle is opened once, mutated once per entry destined for it (bump before rewrites, rewrites in plan.rewrites order), and persisted exactly once -- collapsing what today is N independent open+parse+mutate+persist(atomic_write: temp file + rename + fsync parent + fsync grandparent) cycles into 1. A path that also receives a WorkspaceCargoResolver-routed write to the same root_manifest (VersionWriteTarget::CargoWorkspacePackage or DepWriteTarget::CargoWorkspaceDependency) is excluded from batching entirely via a full pre-scan of plan.bumps and plan.rewrites performed before any mutation begins, and continues to use today's unbatched per-entry immediate open/mutate/persist behavior.",
  "scope": "crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs: apply_version_plan's existing bumps loop and rewrites loop are restructured into (1) a pre-scan/classification pass over plan.bumps and plan.rewrites that partitions Manifest-trait-routed write targets into batchable groups (keyed by path) and a mixed-routing exclusion set (paths also named by a WorkspaceCargoResolver-routed root_manifest), (2) a batched-write pass that opens each batchable path once, applies its bump mutation (if any) then its rewrite mutations in plan.rewrites order, and persists once, and (3) preservation of today's unbatched per-entry open/mutate/persist code path for every Manifest-trait entry whose path is in the exclusion set. A new private/pub(crate) classification function (see api_surface) is added to apply.rs, independently unit-testable from apply_version_plan. The VersionWriteTarget::CargoWorkspacePackage and DepWriteTarget::CargoWorkspaceDependency arms (WorkspaceCargoResolver call sites) are untouched -- they keep calling ws_res.write_version/write_dependency immediately, per entry, exactly as today. crates/callisto-manifests/src/lib.rs: a new persist-call-count instrumentation pair (persist_call_count/reset_persist_call_count), analogous to the existing open_call_count/reset_open_call_count (lib.rs:108-124), backed by its own process-global AtomicUsize distinct from OPEN_CALL_COUNT. crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs, npm.rs, python.rs: each type's persist() implementation (CargoToml::persist, PackageJson::persist, PyprojectToml::persist -- introduced by SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001) increments the new counter on every invocation, success or failure alike is not required -- only successful completion of the write increments it (a failed persist that errors before or during atomic_write does not count as a completed persist). crates/callisto-graph/tests/apply_persist_open_count_test.rs: extended with persist_call_count assertions alongside the existing open_call_count assertions, plus a new #[serial] test in the same file for the mixed-routing exclusion regression (a workspace root Cargo.toml with one plain and one workspace-inherited dependency, both rewritten in the same plan). crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module: new tests for the classification function in isolation (done independent of apply_version_plan), for determinism (bump-before-rewrites, rewrites in plan.rewrites order, on one shared path), for the multi-entry-per-file byte-correctness fixture, and for the partial-failure-mid-batch behavior change. WorkspaceCargoResolver's own internal write_version/write_dependency/persist implementation in crates/callisto-manifests/src/cargo.rs is NOT modified by this spec beyond the persist-counter instrumentation named above, which does not apply to it (WorkspaceCargoResolver never implements the Manifest trait and is not part of the counted persist() calls -- see non_goals).",
  "non_goals": [
    "Reordering apply_version_plan so WorkspaceCargoResolver writes always execute before a mixed path's batched Manifest group. Considered and explicitly rejected in favor of the simpler batching-exclusion approach, to keep this spec's risk profile low after the original combined spec's data-loss ordering bug forced a split into Spec A (SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001) and this deferred spec.",
    "Extending the Spec A mutate/persist split pattern to WorkspaceCargoResolver itself, or adding persist-call-count instrumentation to WorkspaceCargoResolver::persist. WorkspaceCargoResolver-routed writes (VersionWriteTarget::CargoWorkspacePackage, DepWriteTarget::CargoWorkspaceDependency) remain immediate, per-entry, combined mutate+persist, exactly as before this spec, for both batched-eligible and mixed-routing paths.",
    "plan.changelog_writes, plan.consumed_changesets, plan.pre_state_update/delete_pre_json, and lockfile refresh logic in apply.rs -- untouched, same exclusion boundary as Spec A.",
    "Any change to how cascade.rs (crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs) routes edges between DepWriteTarget::Manifest and DepWriteTarget::CargoWorkspaceDependency. That routing and its correctness is locked in by SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001's AC-017 regression test and is a precondition this spec relies on, not something it changes.",
    "Adding a production constructor for VersionWriteTarget::CargoWorkspacePackage. It remains dead/unconstructed in production code paths; this spec's mixed-routing exclusion guard covers it defensively (it is a live, constructible enum variant reachable from hand-built VersionPlan values and from tests) without making it reachable from any real planner output.",
    "Defining batching or exclusion behavior for a VersionPlan where two or more distinct PlannedBump entries' writes both target the same manifest path via VersionWriteTarget::Manifest. The requirement's own language ('the path's bump mutation, if any') presumes at most one bump targets a given canonical manifest path per apply_version_plan call, consistent with each package having exactly one canonical manifest and with SPEC-MANIFEST-PERSIST-001's AC-017 precedent of relying on (rather than re-verifying) upstream planner invariants. No evidence in the source requirement or in cascade.rs indicates this scenario is planner-constructible; this spec does not add a runtime check for it and its behavior (if hand-constructed by a test or a future caller) is left unspecified rather than given an invented, untested resolution.",
    "Any change to Manifest::persist's error type, error mapping, or the atomic_write failure semantics defined in crates/callisto-model/src/atomic.rs. Persist-call-count instrumentation only counts completed successful persists; it does not change what persist() returns or when it fails.",
    "Retrying, resuming, or rolling forward a plan whose batched write for one path failed partway through. The all-or-nothing-per-file behavior change (see AC-006/AC-007) only specifies what apply_version_plan itself leaves on disk and what error it returns; recovery/retry orchestration for the caller is out of scope, unchanged from today."
